4) Backend (Spring Boot) responsibilities

The backend does 3 major jobs:

A) Serve REST APIs for dashboard data

Examples:

list insights

top insights by savings

CRUD endpoints for the primary table (insights)

These endpoints return JSON DTOs (I used Java records for clean data objects).

B) Join tables to return “display-ready” responses

Instead of returning raw IDs, backend returns:

twin: { id, name, address }

creator: { id, name, position }

So the frontend can show “Twin Name” and “Creator Name”, not numeric IDs.

C) Copilot API

Backend also provides:

POST /api/copilot/ask

This endpoint:

gathers context from DB

optionally calls the LLM (Ollama/Mistral)

returns final answer text

5) DTOs (Java records) — why you used them

Example record you showed:

public record InsightResponse(
  Long id,
  String insightName,
  Double projectedSavingsUsd,
  String assignee,
  LocalDateTime dateClosed,
  LocalDateTime lastActive,
  TwinDto twin,
  CreatorDto creator
) {
  public record TwinDto(Long id, String name, String address) {}
  public record CreatorDto(Long id, String name, String position) {}
}

Why records are good here

no boilerplate getters/setters

immutable (safe API responses)

clean JSON serialization

perfect for service → controller → response

6)  backend technical challenges you solved
A) Column mismatch errors

 originally queried i.savings, i.status, i.created_at, but the schema actually uses:

projected_savings_usd

(no status column)

no created_at column

I corrected the SQL to match the real schema.

B) BigDecimal vs Double

Postgres numeric(12,2) returns BigDecimal, not Double.
So I updated Java types to BigDecimal for money and updated aggregation logic.



